        "scanners": {
          "vt": {
            "status": "clean",
            "verdict": "benign",
            "normalizedStatus": "clean",
            "analysis": "Type: OpenClaw Skill\nName: sherpamind\nVersion: 0.1.3\n\nSherpaMind is a legitimate local-first integration for SherpaDesk that provides data ingestion, synchronization, and retrieval-augmented generation (RAG) capabilities. The bundle includes a Python-based CLI and a background sync service managed via systemd user units (src/sherpamind/service_manager.py). While the tool handles sensitive API credentials and establishes persistence for background tasks, these behaviors are transparently documented in SKILL.md and README.md as core features of the system. The code is well-structured, includes a comprehensive test suite, and shows no evidence of malicious intent, obfuscation, or unauthorized data exfiltration.",
            "source": "palm",
            "checkedAt": 1774120909368
          },
          "skillspector": null,
          "llm": {
            "status": "suspicious",
            "verdict": "suspicious",
            "normalizedStatus": "suspicious",
            "confidence": "medium",
            "summary": "The skill appears to implement a real local SherpaDesk backend (not obviously malicious) but the package metadata omits critical sensitive requirements (API credentials and service/installation behavior) and the bundle can create local state, store credentials, install a venv, and optionally install a user-level service — mismatches that merit caution before installing.",
            "dimensions": [
              {
                "detail": "The SKILL.md and README clearly state this is a local SherpaDesk backend that requires SherpaDesk API credentials and will create workspace-local runtime state, a venv, and optionally a user-level systemd service. Registry metadata, however, lists no required environment variables, no primary credential, and calls the skill instruction-only — that is inconsistent. Requesting SherpaDesk credentials is coherent with the stated purpose, but those credentials should have been declared in the skill metadata.",
                "label": "Purpose & Capability",
                "name": "purpose_capability",
                "rating": "concern"
              },
              {
                "detail": "Runtime instructions explicitly direct the agent to run the bundled Python scripts (scripts/run.py) from the repo root, create .SherpaMind/ state including SQLite DB and public artifacts, install a Python venv and PyPI deps during bootstrap, store SherpaDesk credentials in .SherpaMind/private/config.env, and optionally install/start a user-level systemd service. All of this is within the claimed scope, but it includes persistent local state and credentials storage which users should be aware of and verify.",
                "label": "Instruction Scope",
                "name": "instruction_scope",
                "rating": "note"
              },
              {
                "detail": "No install spec is provided in the registry (instruction-only), yet the bundle includes bootstrap/run scripts and pyproject/requirements for installing Python dependencies from PyPI. That means the skill will perform runtime installation (venv + pip) when bootstrapped rather than via a tracked install step — not inherently malicious, but less transparent and raises supply-chain review needs (which PyPI packages will be installed should be vetted).",
                "label": "Install Mechanism",
                "name": "install_mechanism",
                "rating": "note"
              },
              {
                "detail": "SKILL.md and README require three sensitive env vars (SHERPADESK_API_KEY, SHERPADESK_ORG_KEY, SHERPADESK_INSTANCE_KEY) and say credentials may be stored locally, but the skill registry metadata declares no required env vars or primary credential. This mismatch reduces visibility and is a material concern because the skill will hold and use account-level API keys and persist them in the workspace.",
                "label": "Credentials",
                "name": "environment_proportionality",
                "rating": "concern"
              },
              {
                "detail": "The skill can create workspace-local persistent state (.SherpaMind/private/...), create a venv, and optionally install a user-level systemd service that runs a background sync/enrichment process. That persistent background capability combined with credential access increases blast radius if abused. The skill does not request system-wide privileges, and always:false is set, but the ability to install a user service plus local credential storage warrants caution.",
                "label": "Persistence & Privilege",
                "name": "persistence_privilege",
                "rating": "concern"
